WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.870
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.990
build a growable collection using a backing

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.965
array, logical size, and ordered copying.

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:13.829
Applications often collect more records than

00:00:13.829 --> 00:00:14.989
they start with.

00:00:14.989 --> 00:00:18.397
Understanding how storage grows helps you build

00:00:18.397 --> 00:00:21.587
reusable collections that accept new entries

00:00:21.587 --> 00:00:24.704
without losing earlier data or changing its

00:00:24.704 --> 00:00:25.139
order.

00:00:25.812 --> 00:00:29.032
Today we'll help a campus signup coordinator

00:00:29.032 --> 00:00:31.228
record names in arrival order.

00:00:31.228 --> 00:00:34.741
We'll start with room for two names, then accept

00:00:34.741 --> 00:00:35.107
more.

00:00:35.107 --> 00:00:38.620
Each added name counts as one entry, and earlier

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.987
signups must stay in their original positions.

00:00:42.664 --> 00:00:45.446
We'll track used entries separately from

00:00:45.446 --> 00:00:48.784
available slots, make more room when needed, and

00:00:48.784 --> 00:00:51.009
print the names in signup order.

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:54.000
A worked report will show size and capacity

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.669
before and after growth.

00:00:55.669 --> 00:00:57.964
Then you'll predict a fresh case.

00:00:58.018 --> 00:01:01.466
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:01.466 --> 00:01:02.268
Main.java.

00:01:06.498 --> 00:01:09.595
Our collection needs storage for names and a

00:01:09.595 --> 00:01:11.847
count of entries actually added.

00:01:11.847 --> 00:01:14.944
Let's define GrowingNames, keep those fields

00:01:14.944 --> 00:01:18.323
private, and initialize them in its constructor.

00:01:33.276 --> 00:01:36.576
Storage refers to the backing array, the array

00:01:36.576 --> 00:01:38.441
our class uses internally.

00:01:38.441 --> 00:01:41.813
Its length is capacity: the number of available

00:01:41.813 --> 00:01:42.244
slots.

00:01:42.244 --> 00:01:45.329
Size is the number of entries added so far.

00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:48.270
We begin with two slots but zero entries.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.642
New String array slots contain null, meaning no

00:01:51.642 --> 00:01:54.727
object reference; those empty slots are not

00:01:54.727 --> 00:01:55.301
signups.

00:01:55.317 --> 00:01:57.796
An array cannot change its length.

00:01:57.796 --> 00:02:01.223
Our add method therefore needs to detect a full

00:02:01.223 --> 00:02:04.723
array and allocate a larger one before inserting

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:05.671
another name.

00:02:05.671 --> 00:02:07.567
Let's write that decision.

00:02:20.348 --> 00:02:23.772
When size equals storage length, every current

00:02:23.772 --> 00:02:24.740
slot is used.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.941
Larger refers to a new array with twice the

00:02:27.941 --> 00:02:28.611
capacity.

00:02:28.611 --> 00:02:31.886
That allocation creates room, but it has not

00:02:31.886 --> 00:02:33.077
moved any names.

00:02:33.077 --> 00:02:36.352
The original storage still holds the earlier

00:02:36.352 --> 00:02:36.948
signups.

00:02:36.983 --> 00:02:40.278
We must preserve those signups before replacing

00:02:40.278 --> 00:02:41.821
the storage reference.

00:02:41.821 --> 00:02:45.116
Let's copy each used entry to the same index in

00:02:45.116 --> 00:02:48.061
the larger array, then make that array our

00:02:48.061 --> 00:02:49.183
backing storage.

00:03:05.842 --> 00:03:09.145
The loop visits indexes from zero up to, but not

00:03:09.145 --> 00:03:10.246
including, size.

00:03:10.246 --> 00:03:13.274
Each assignment reads from the old array and

00:03:13.274 --> 00:03:16.509
writes to the matching position in the new one.

00:03:16.509 --> 00:03:19.812
After the loop, storage is reassigned to larger.

00:03:19.812 --> 00:03:22.771
Copying changes capacity, not the number of

00:03:22.771 --> 00:03:25.042
signups, so size stays unchanged.

00:03:25.068 --> 00:03:28.020
Now there is space for the incoming name,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.396
whether growth was needed or not.

00:03:30.396 --> 00:03:33.708
Let's store it at the next unused position and

00:03:33.708 --> 00:03:35.868
then increase the entry count.

00:03:41.579 --> 00:03:44.944
Before insertion, size is also the index of the

00:03:44.944 --> 00:03:46.161
next unused slot.

00:03:46.161 --> 00:03:48.882
We write there first and increase size

00:03:48.882 --> 00:03:49.598
afterward.

00:03:49.598 --> 00:03:52.963
Reversing those steps would skip a position and

00:03:52.963 --> 00:03:55.039
could write beyond the array.

00:03:55.039 --> 00:03:58.117
The growth check stays inside add, so every

00:03:58.117 --> 00:04:01.554
caller gets the same ordered insertion behavior.

00:04:01.577 --> 00:04:04.860
The coordinator also needs to read a name and

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.318
inspect both counts.

00:04:06.318 --> 00:04:09.820
Let's expose those values through public methods

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:12.227
while keeping the fields private.

00:04:29.525 --> 00:04:32.691
Get returns the entry at its supplied index.

00:04:32.691 --> 00:04:36.144
This small implementation assumes callers choose

00:04:36.144 --> 00:04:39.310
an index from zero up to, but not including,

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:42.331
size; it does not check that precondition.

00:04:42.331 --> 00:04:45.281
Size returns the number of added entries.

00:04:45.281 --> 00:04:47.439
Capacity returns array length.

00:04:47.439 --> 00:04:50.533
Keeping those counts separate lets a report

00:04:50.533 --> 00:04:51.900
avoid unused slots.

00:04:52.092 --> 00:04:54.641
The collection definition is ready.

00:04:54.641 --> 00:04:57.992
In Main, the main method will start our worked

00:04:57.992 --> 00:04:58.575
example.

00:04:58.575 --> 00:05:01.925
Let's create an empty collection and print its

00:05:01.925 --> 00:05:03.892
starting size and capacity.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:26.489
Constructing GrowingNames runs the constructor

00:05:26.489 --> 00:05:26.859
once.

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:30.046
The report should show zero entries and two

00:05:30.046 --> 00:05:30.491
slots.

00:05:30.491 --> 00:05:33.826
We'll now add Maya and Luis in that order and

00:05:33.826 --> 00:05:35.530
inspect the full state.

00:05:49.706 --> 00:05:53.545
Maya occupies index zero and Luis occupies index

00:05:53.545 --> 00:05:53.865
one.

00:05:53.865 --> 00:05:57.463
After those calls, size reaches two, matching

00:05:57.463 --> 00:05:58.183
capacity.

00:05:58.183 --> 00:06:01.862
Neither call needed a larger array: each had a

00:06:01.862 --> 00:06:03.781
free slot when it began.

00:06:03.816 --> 00:06:05.174
Nora arrives next.

00:06:05.174 --> 00:06:08.719
Let's add her name, report the grown state, and

00:06:08.719 --> 00:06:12.038
print only the names that have actually been

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:12.491
added.

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:38.727
Nora's call begins with a full array.

00:06:38.727 --> 00:06:41.759
Add makes room, copies Maya and Luis, and

00:06:41.759 --> 00:06:43.534
inserts Nora after them.

00:06:43.534 --> 00:06:46.936
The reporting loop stops at size, so an unused

00:06:46.936 --> 00:06:49.673
slot cannot appear as another signup.

00:06:49.673 --> 00:06:53.075
Let's click Run to inspect both the counts and

00:06:53.075 --> 00:06:53.815
the order.

00:07:00.556 --> 00:07:03.426
Start shows zero entries in two slots.

00:07:03.426 --> 00:07:06.144
Full shows two entries in two slots.

00:07:06.144 --> 00:07:09.467
Grown shows three entries in four slots: the

00:07:09.467 --> 00:07:12.940
third addition doubled capacity but added only

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:13.620
one name.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.867
Maya, Luis, and Nora still print in arrival

00:07:16.867 --> 00:07:17.320
order.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:20.643
The spare fourth slot is omitted because the

00:07:20.643 --> 00:07:22.606
loop follows logical size.

00:07:25.058 --> 00:07:28.146
Let's try a fresh collection with Iris, Owen,

00:07:28.146 --> 00:07:29.038
Bea, and Kai.

00:07:29.038 --> 00:07:32.126
We'll enter the same class again, then change

00:07:32.126 --> 00:07:32.881
the caller.

00:07:32.881 --> 00:07:36.038
Trace when each addition finds a free slot and

00:07:36.038 --> 00:07:38.508
when it must grow the backing array.

00:08:30.906 --> 00:08:34.080
The constructor and copy logic are in place.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.038
Let's complete insertion and the readers.

00:08:37.038 --> 00:08:40.212
Remember that growing storage alone must not

00:08:40.212 --> 00:08:43.531
increase size; adding the new entry does that.

00:09:09.176 --> 00:09:12.586
Now let's create the fresh collection in Main

00:09:12.586 --> 00:09:14.328
and add the four names.

00:09:14.328 --> 00:09:17.965
Each call sees the size and capacity left by the

00:09:17.965 --> 00:09:19.026
previous call.

00:09:53.354 --> 00:09:55.675
The four additions are entered.

00:09:55.675 --> 00:09:58.969
Let's finish with separate size and capacity

00:09:58.969 --> 00:10:01.964
lines, followed by the entries in order.

00:10:01.964 --> 00:10:05.258
This report checks both storage behavior and

00:10:05.258 --> 00:10:07.429
preservation of earlier data.

00:10:37.754 --> 00:10:41.078
Before running, predict all six output lines.

00:10:41.078 --> 00:10:44.401
Identify which addition needs a larger array,

00:10:44.401 --> 00:10:47.651
then decide whether the final addition fits.

00:10:47.651 --> 00:10:51.122
Also predict the order of the names and explain

00:10:51.122 --> 00:10:53.117
why no unused slot appears.

00:10:53.117 --> 00:10:55.554
Pause here if you need more time.

00:11:04.318 --> 00:11:07.761
Let's click Run and compare the actual report

00:11:07.761 --> 00:11:09.368
with your prediction.

00:11:19.544 --> 00:11:21.977
Size is four and capacity is four.

00:11:21.977 --> 00:11:25.198
Bea's third addition found the two-slot array

00:11:25.198 --> 00:11:27.416
full and grew it to four slots.

00:11:27.416 --> 00:11:30.708
Kai's fourth addition used the remaining slot,

00:11:30.708 --> 00:11:32.497
so it did not grow again.

00:11:32.497 --> 00:11:35.861
Iris, Owen, Bea, and Kai print in order because

00:11:35.861 --> 00:11:39.010
copying preserved their indexes and each new

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:41.944
name was appended after the used entries.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:46.222
The signup collection can now accept more

00:11:46.222 --> 00:11:49.288
entries than its starting array could hold.

00:11:49.288 --> 00:11:52.495
Logical size identifies the entries, capacity

00:11:52.495 --> 00:11:55.703
measures available space, and ordered copying

00:11:55.703 --> 00:11:57.556
preserves earlier records.

00:11:57.556 --> 00:12:00.906
The public add method keeps the grow, copy, and

00:12:00.906 --> 00:12:03.900
append sequence together for every caller.

00:12:05.226 --> 00:12:08.337
If Zoe signs up next, what size and capacity

00:12:08.337 --> 00:12:11.590
should the report show, and which index should

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:12.580
hold her name?

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:15.762
Explain which entries would be copied and why

00:12:15.762 --> 00:12:19.086
the reporting loop should still use size rather

00:12:19.086 --> 00:12:20.076
than capacity.
